.TH CTYPE 3
.SH NAME
isalpha, isupper, islower, isdigit, isxdigit, isalnum, isspace, isblank, ispunct,
isprint, isgraph, iscntrl, isascii, toupper, tolower \-
character classification and case conversion
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <ctype.h>
.PP
.B int isalpha(int c);
.PP
.B . . .
.PP
.B int toupper(int c);
.PP
.B int tolower(int c);
.SH DESCRIPTION
These macros classify ASCII-coded integer values
by table lookup.
Each is a predicate returning nonzero for true,
zero for false.
.I Isascii
is defined on all integer values; the rest
are defined only where
.I isascii
is true and on the single non-ASCII value
EOF (see
.IR stdio (3)).
.TP 15n
.I isalpha
.I c
is a letter
.TP
.I isupper
.I c
is an upper case letter
.TP
.I islower
.I c
is a lower case letter
.TP
.I isdigit
.I c
is a digit
.TP
.I isxdigit
.I c
is a hexadecimal digit
.TP
.I isalnum
.I c
is an alphanumeric character
.TP
.I isspace
.I c
is a space, tab, carriage return, newline, vertical tab or formfeed
.TP
.I isblank
.I c
is a space or a tab.
.B Note:
C11; v7 had no such test.
.TP
.I ispunct
.I c
is a punctuation character (neither control nor alphanumeric)
.TP
.I isprint
.I c
is a printing character, code 040(8) (space) through 0176 (tilde)
.TP
.I isgraph
.I c
is a printing character other than the space
.TP
.I iscntrl
.I c
is a delete character (0177) or ordinary control character
(less than 040).
.TP
.I isascii
.I c
is an ASCII character, code less than 0200
.PP
.I Toupper
and
.I tolower
convert a letter to the other case and return
.B any other argument unchanged.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
ascii(7), string(3)
.SH FILES
share/besm6/lib/libc.a
.br
lib/libc/gen/ctype_.c, toupper.c, tolower.c, isblank.c, include/ctype.h
.SH BESM-6 NOTES
.SS The table is v7's, unchanged, because this terminal is ASCII
Not KOI7.  The console driver passes bytes untranslated in both directions, so
there was nothing in the classification to re-derive.
.SS isprint(' ') is true now, and it was not in v7
v7 left bit 0200 of its table free.  It carries
.B _B
on the space here, which is what makes
.I isprint
and
.I isgraph
distinguishable \- v7's
.I isprint
answered
.I false
for a space, which is a plain violation of C11 sec 7.4.1.8.
.SS toupper and tolower could not stay macros
v7's are
.I unconditional
\-
.B "(c) \- 'a' + 'A'"
\- so v7's
.I toupper('1')
returns garbage, where C11 sec 7.4.2 requires the argument back unchanged when it
is not a character of the other case.  The conditional form must test the class
and then yield the argument, that is,
.I evaluate it twice,
which sec 7.1.4 forbids of a macro.
.PP
So both are real functions here, and
.B v7's unconditional pair keeps the name v7 itself gave it on other machines:
.B _toupper and _tolower.
A caller that has already checked the class can still have the cheap one.
.PP
.I Isblank
went the same way, for want of a bit rather than for the fold: it is the space
and the tab, and the tab's only class is _S, which also covers newline, vertical
tab, form feed and carriage return.  A macro would have had to test the two
characters separately.
.SS The table is 129 entries, not 128
Every macro indexes
.BR "(_ctype_ + 1)[c]" ,
so EOF (\-1) lands on a leading zero and classifies as nothing.
.PP
.B char is unsigned here,
so a byte of 0200..0377 is reachable and runs off the end of the table.  Two
places in the tree had to be fixed for that:
.IR strtol (3)'s
scanner, and a
.IR isspace ()
in libcurses applied to a window cell carrying a standout bit.
.SS The whole table is 22 words
Six chars to a word.
.SS The functions are declared but not all defined
C11 sec 7.4 wants each classification to exist as a function as well as a macro,
so that
.B (isalpha)(c)
and
.B &isalpha
work.  <ctype.h> declares them all;
.IR toupper ,
.I tolower
and
.I isblank
are the three that exist.  The rest are a TODO, and the macros serve every caller
in this tree today.
